Reviewers agree the paper tackles an important systems problem, i.e., making learned indexes practically updatable, and find the sigmoid-boosting idea and empirical gains (retraining cost, QPS, memory) promising. 

However, they raise substantial concerns about clarity and rigor: 

1. Lookup/update procedures and notation are hard to follow; 

2. The motivation and advantages of sigmoids over piecewise-linear models and simple scaling are not convincingly justified; 

3. The preservation of ε-bounded error is only informally argued and partly enforced via fallback search. 

Questions also remain about baseline coverage (missing newer 2024–25 LIs), memory/QPS reporting details, and sensitivity to hyperparameters and workload shifts, especially in real deployments.
